WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.460
Bash scripts frequently work with files and directories.

2
00:00:03.560 --> 00:00:14.840
They read logs, copy artifacts, extract archives, process uploads, write reports, synchronize directories, clean temporary workspaces, and collect diagnostics.

3
00:00:14.940 --> 00:00:20.140
That makes path handling one of the most important security concerns in shell automation.

4
00:00:20.240 --> 00:00:28.160
A script that chooses the wrong file can expose data, overwrite a result, pollute a workspace, or act outside the area the operator expected.

5
00:00:28.440 --> 00:00:33.560
A path becomes security-relevant input as soon as it influences a file operation.

6
00:00:33.660 --> 00:00:43.900
The value may come from a command-line argument, a configuration file, an environment variable, CI/CD metadata, tool output, an archive manifest, or a generated filename.

7
00:00:44.000 --> 00:00:54.960
It may not look like user input in the traditional sense, but it still crosses a trust boundary when the script decides what to read, write, copy, extract, synchronize, or clean.

8
00:00:55.240 --> 00:01:02.120
Directory traversal prevention means ensuring that path input cannot move the script outside the locations it is supposed to use.

9
00:01:02.220 --> 00:01:05.500
The point is not to distrust every filename for no reason.

10
00:01:05.600 --> 00:01:10.560
The point is to make file operations intentional, scoped, and reviewable.

11
00:01:10.660 --> 00:01:16.740
A script should know where its approved input, staging, output, extraction, and cleanup areas are before it acts.

12
00:01:16.840 --> 00:01:22.440
If a path does not fit that scope, the script should fail safely before touching the filesystem.

13
00:01:22.720 --> 00:01:26.400
This control reduces both security and reliability risk.

14
00:01:26.500 --> 00:01:36.180
It helps prevent accidental reads from sensitive areas, writes into unintended destinations, extraction outside a controlled directory, and cleanup in the wrong place.

15
00:01:36.280 --> 00:01:42.920
It also makes code review easier because reviewers can see how the script defines its safe operating area.

16
00:01:43.020 --> 00:01:51.820
Good path handling gives Bash automation a clear boundary: use approved locations, reject unsupported path input, and make file behavior predictable.

17
00:01:51.920 --> 00:01:57.760
Reviewers can verify how the script defines and enforces its safe filesystem operating area.
